`scripts/mapping_scratchpad.js` is a **Three.js scratchpad**, not runnable as-is: the top block references `radius` / `geometry` that are never defined, `wireframeMaterial` is declared twice, and district/click code assumes meshes you are skipping. Below is how the **intended flow** works if you wire only the asteroid into a container.

## What you need in HTML first

```html
<div id="asteroid-map-container" style="width: 400px; height: 400px;"></div>
<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.160.0/build/three.min.js"></script>
<script>
  // your JS (adapted from the scratchpad) goes here
</script>
```

Three.js draws into a **`<canvas>`** that the renderer creates; you either leave that canvas in the div (live view) or copy one frame into an `<img>` (static PNG).

---

## Step 1 — Distort an icosahedron into a lumpy asteroid (lines 1–18)

Concept:

1. Start with `THREE.IcosahedronGeometry(radius, detail)` — a low-poly sphere (detail `1` ≈ 20 faces after subdivision).
2. Walk every vertex in `geometry.attributes.position`.
3. Scale each vertex by a noise factor so the sphere becomes irregular.

Minimal version (fixing the scratchpad’s missing variables):

```javascript
const radius = 4;
const asteroidGeom = new THREE.IcosahedronGeometry(radius, 1);
const positionAttribute = asteroidGeom.attributes.position;

for (let i = 0; i < positionAttribute.count; i++) {
  const x = positionAttribute.getX(i);
  const y = positionAttribute.getY(i);
  const z = positionAttribute.getZ(i);
  const noise = 0.8 + Math.random() * 0.4; // scratchpad: deterministic-ish lump
  positionAttribute.setXYZ(i, x * noise, y * noise, z * noise);
}
positionAttribute.needsUpdate = true;
```

Use **one** geometry + **one** material for the asteroid only; skip lines 60–67 (district meshes) and 81–114 (raycaster / click).

---

## Step 2 — Bind to your container and create scene/camera/renderer (lines 26–45)

```javascript
const container = document.getElementById('asteroid-map-container');
const width = container.clientWidth;
const height = container.clientHeight;

const scene = new THREE.Scene();
scene.background = new THREE.Color(0x000000);

const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(75, width / height, 0.1, 1000);
camera.position.set(0, 0, 10); // pull camera back so the mesh is in frame

const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true });
renderer.setSize(width, height);
container.appendChild(renderer.domElement); // canvas lives inside the div
```

The container’s **CSS width/height** define aspect ratio and pixel size; `clientWidth` / `clientHeight` must be non-zero when you run this (the div needs explicit or layout-derived size).

---

## Step 3 — Material + mesh + add to scene (lines 47–58, asteroid only)

```javascript
const wireframeMaterial = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({
  color: 0x00ff00,
  wireframe: true,
});

const asteroidMesh = new THREE.Mesh(asteroidGeom, wireframeMaterial);
scene.add(asteroidMesh);
// do not add district meshes; do not use districtsArray / onMouseClick
```

---

## Step 4 — Draw once: “display the image” (lines 69–71)

```javascript
renderer.render(scene, camera);
```

That paints **one frame** into the canvas inside `#asteroid-map-container`. For a static wireframe asteroid, that is enough.

If the container can resize, also handle resize (not in the scratchpad):

```javascript
window.addEventListener('resize', () => {
  const w = container.clientWidth;
  const h = container.clientHeight;
  camera.aspect = w / h;
  camera.updateProjectionMatrix();
  renderer.setSize(w, h);
  renderer.render(scene, camera);
});
```

Optional: `requestAnimationFrame` loop only if you want spin or animation later.

---

## Alternative: static `<img>` instead of keeping the canvas (lines 74–78)

The scratchpad’s second path:

```javascript
renderer.render(scene, camera);
const imgDataUrl = renderer.domElement.toDataURL('image/png');
document.getElementById('my-static-map-image').src = imgDataUrl;
```

HTML:

```html
<img id="my-static-map-image" alt="Asteroid map" />
```

Use this when you want a normal image element (e.g. embed in Dendry `{! … !}`) and do not need interaction on the canvas. You still need Three.js + renderer once to **generate** the PNG; you can remove the canvas from the DOM afterward if you only want the `<img>`.

For “show in container,” the **canvas-in-div** path (step 4) is usually simpler.

---

## End-to-end minimal script (asteroid only)

```javascript
const container = document.getElementById('asteroid-map-container');
const width = container.clientWidth;
const height = container.clientHeight;

const scene = new THREE.Scene();
scene.background = new THREE.Color(0x000000);

const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(75, width / height, 0.1, 1000);
camera.position.set(0, 0, 10);

const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true });
renderer.setSize(width, height);
container.appendChild(renderer.domElement);

const asteroidGeom = new THREE.IcosahedronGeometry(4, 1);
const pos = asteroidGeom.attributes.position;
for (let i = 0; i < pos.count; i++) {
  const noise = 0.8 + Math.random() * 0.4;
  pos.setXYZ(
    i,
    pos.getX(i) * noise,
    pos.getY(i) * noise,
    pos.getZ(i) * noise
  );
}
pos.needsUpdate = true;

const material = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color: 0x00ff00, wireframe: true });
scene.add(new THREE.Mesh(asteroidGeom, material));

renderer.render(scene, camera);
```

---

## Map scratchpad sections → what you use

| Lines | Use for asteroid-only? |
|-------|-------------------------|
| 1–18 | Yes — vertex noise on `IcosahedronGeometry` |
| 20–23 | Duplicate of 47–51; pick one material block |
| 26–45 | Yes — container, scene, camera, renderer, append canvas |
| 47–58 | Yes — asteroid mesh only |
| 60–67 | No — districts |
| 69–71 | Yes — `render()` to show it |
| 74–78 | Optional — PNG → `<img>` instead of visible canvas |
| 81–114 | No — clicking / raycaster |

---

## Practical notes

1. **Load Three.js** before your script (`THREE` global or ES module import).
2. **Random shape**: `Math.random()` in the loop gives a new lump every reload; for a stable map, seed a PRNG or use simplex/Perlin noise (as the comment on line 13 suggests).
3. **Not a photo**: this is procedural wireframe 3D, not `img/asteroid.jpg` from card art.
4. **Mikasa game UI**: if this goes in a `.scene.dry` card, you’d typically run similar JS in `{! … !}` on `on-display:` and ensure the container exists in that page’s HTML (or create the div in the same block).

If you say whether you want **live canvas in a div** vs **one PNG in an `<img>`** inside the Dendry sidebar, the wiring into `game.js` / a specific scene can be spelled out next.

